From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] autoapp: install/remove the input event filter on the GUI thread

InputDevice::start()/stop() are AASDK service lifecycle callbacks, invoked
from whichever boost::asio worker thread happens to handle the relevant
service binding/teardown message -- not the Qt GUI thread. `parent_` (the
QApplication instance) and `this` (moved to parent_'s thread in the
InputDevice constructor) both live on the GUI thread, but
parent_.installEventFilter(this)/removeEventFilter(this) directly mutate
parent_'s internal event-filter list, which the GUI thread's event loop
concurrently walks on every single dispatched event. Calling those methods
from a different thread than the one both objects live on is an
unsynchronized cross-thread QObject access.

This was diagnosed on-device after the 0007 native-touch-event fix still
left touch completely non-responsive: the "[InputDevice] start." log line
(and the AASDK binding-request/status logs around it) is emitted on an
io_service worker thread, visibly different from the thread that logs
"[QtVideoOutput] create." (a GUI-thread object created during app startup).
The 0007 patch's eventFilter() diagnostic -- logged unconditionally for
every event the filter sees -- never fired even once during an active
Android Auto session with the touchscreen being tapped repeatedly, meaning
installEventFilter() silently had no effect: the cross-thread call does not
trip Qt's same-thread assertion (that check compares parent_'s thread
against the filter object's thread, which do match thanks to the
constructor's moveToThread -- it says nothing about which thread is doing
the calling), so it neither warns nor crashes, it just races with the GUI
thread's own iteration of that same list and loses.

Route both calls through QMetaObject::invokeMethod(this, ..., Qt::
QueuedConnection) so they actually run on `this`'s (i.e. the GUI thread's)
event loop instead of executing inline on the calling io_service thread.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: works around a cross-thread
QObject::installEventFilter/removeEventFilter race between this project's
boost::asio service-callback threads and the Qt GUI thread, observed on
this board's EGLFS backend while diagnosing touch being completely
non-responsive]

diff --git a/src/autoapp/Projection/InputDevice.cpp b/src/autoapp/Projection/InputDevice.cpp
index 1fff0c2..8a2c9e1 100644
--- a/src/autoapp/Projection/InputDevice.cpp
+++ b/src/autoapp/Projection/InputDevice.cpp
@@ -45,7 +45,25 @@ void InputDevice::start(IInputDeviceEventHandler& eventHandler)

     OPENAUTO_LOG(info) << "[InputDevice] start.";
     eventHandler_ = &eventHandler;
-    parent_.installEventFilter(this);
+
+    // start() runs on whichever boost::asio worker thread happens to handle
+    // the AASDK service binding callback -- NOT the Qt GUI thread that both
+    // `this` (moved to parent's thread in the constructor) and `parent_`
+    // (the QApplication instance) actually live on. installEventFilter()
+    // mutates parent_'s internal event-filter list, which the GUI thread's
+    // event loop concurrently reads on every dispatched event; calling it
+    // directly from a non-GUI thread is an unsynchronized cross-thread
+    // QObject access, confirmed here by diagnostic logging: "[InputDevice]
+    // start." fires on the io_service thread while "[QtVideoOutput] create."
+    // (a GUI-thread object) fires on a different thread id, and the
+    // eventFilter() diagnostic added to trace touch delivery never logs a
+    // single call afterwards -- i.e. the filter silently never takes effect,
+    // which is exactly the symptom (touch completely non-responsive) all the
+    // preceding geometry/native-touch-event fixes did not resolve. Queue the
+    // actual installation onto `this`'s own (GUI) thread instead.
+    QMetaObject::invokeMethod(this, [this]() {
+        parent_.installEventFilter(this);
+    }, Qt::QueuedConnection);
 }

 void InputDevice::stop()
@@ -53,8 +71,12 @@ void InputDevice::stop()
     std::lock_guard<decltype(mutex_)> lock(mutex_);

     OPENAUTO_LOG(info) << "[InputDevice] stop.";
-    parent_.removeEventFilter(this);
     eventHandler_ = nullptr;
+
+    // See start() -- same cross-thread hazard applies to removeEventFilter().
+    QMetaObject::invokeMethod(this, [this]() {
+        parent_.removeEventFilter(this);
+    }, Qt::QueuedConnection);
 }

 bool InputDevice::eventFilter(QObject* obj, QEvent* event)
